# Tier C rule-neutral SUBSET — frozen 2026-08-03. Derived, NOT re-measured.
#
# PURPOSE: the instance set for T3's parity half (plan §8.3) — 'instances where the
# phase-4 rules we still LACK do not materially help the baselines, verified by
# baseline ablation'. Score BOTH baselines (kissat AND cadical) on this subset.
#
# DERIVATION: window `20260727-002213-C-kabl` (git 5ba6cda, proof-free ablation array).
# An instance is EXCLUDED iff ablating Sweep changes EITHER baseline's runtime by
# >25%: max(kissat --sweep=0 / kissat, cadical --sweep=false / cadical) > 1.25.
# Sweep is the dominant technique we still lack (+14.00 PAR-2 to kissat; the other
# four are -0.67..+7.91 — L11). 24 of 30 instances are kept; the 6 dropped are listed
# at the foot with their ratios.
#
# ▶ KNOWN DEFECT — READ BEFORE TRUSTING A VERDICT ON THIS SUBSET.
# The ablation is SINGLE-SHOT (one run per arm), so a ratio cannot distinguish
# 'sweep matters here' from 'this instance is chaotic'. FOUR of the six exclusions
# (ContextModel, j3037, jgiraldezlevy, urq45) are on the measured perturbation-FRAGILE
# list (probe 1579386: 8-23x swings under walk/bump/chrono, none of which is sweep),
# and their sweep ratios (1.54-2.54) sit INSIDE those bands. Only em_11 (both baselines
# move, 2.65x/2.30x) and DLTM (kissat 3.57x) survive that objection.
# CONSEQUENCE: the subset excludes exactly the instances where variable retirement
# costs Tier C, so 'R-d helps on the subset' may be an artefact of this defect, not a
# finding. Do not bank that conclusion off this list.
# CLOSER: re-run the sweep ablation with >=3 repeats per arm and re-derive; an
# exclusion should require the sweep delta to exceed the instance's own replicate band.
#
# A max-over-five-lacked-techniques criterion was TESTED AND REJECTED: it excludes 16
# of 30 and is plainly noise-amplifying (mp1-Nb5T15 shows 10.18x from --eagersubsume=0,
# a technique worth +5.94 PAR-2 across the WHOLE tier).

